<p class="gna-lede">A broken cheekbone (zygomatic fracture) usually follows a direct blow to the side of the face. The tell-tale signs are a flattened cheek, numbness of the cheek or upper lip, bruising around the eye and difficulty opening the mouth. Fractures that have not moved often heal without surgery; those that change the shape of the face, affect the eye or limit mouth opening are usually repaired with small plates, ideally within about two weeks.</p>

<h2 id="what-is-zygomatic-fracture">What is a zygomatic (cheekbone) fracture?</h2>
<p>The zygoma is the cheekbone. It gives the cheek its prominence, forms the outer wall and part of the floor of the eye socket, and connects to the rest of the skull at four points: the forehead bone beside the eyebrow, the upper jaw beside the nose, the floor of the eye socket and a thin bridge of bone in front of the ear called the zygomatic arch.</p>
<p>There are two main patterns of injury:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Zygomatic arch fracture:</strong> the thin bridge in front of the ear breaks and is pushed inwards. This leaves a dent at the side of the face and can block the movement of the lower jaw.</li>
  <li><strong>Zygomaticomaxillary complex (ZMC) fracture:</strong> sometimes called a "tripod" fracture, where the whole cheekbone breaks away at its joins and is pushed inwards, backwards or downwards. Because the cheekbone forms part of the eye socket, these fractures often involve the orbit too.</li>
</ul>
<p>In India the most common causes are road-traffic accidents, particularly two-wheeler falls, followed by assaults, falls at home or work, and sports injuries from cricket balls or hockey sticks.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A flat cheek, a numb upper lip and difficulty opening the mouth are the classic signs of a broken cheekbone.</li>
    <li>Swelling can hide a flattened cheek for several days, so a review once it settles is important.</li>
    <li>Undisplaced fractures usually heal without surgery; displaced fractures are often fixed with small titanium plates.</li>
    <li>When surgery is needed, it is usually done within about two weeks, before the bone sets in the wrong position.</li>
    <li>Because the cheekbone forms part of the eye socket, an eye check is part of every assessment.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="signs">Signs of a broken cheekbone</h2>
<p>Pain, swelling and bruising are expected after any facial injury. The signs that point towards a fracture are:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A flattened cheek</strong> compared with the other side. It is often masked by swelling at first and becomes obvious a few days later.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness or tingling</strong> of the cheek, side of the nose, upper lip, and upper teeth and gum on the injured side. The infraorbital nerve passes through the cheekbone and is easily bruised or pinched.</li>
  <li><strong>Difficulty or pain opening the mouth</strong>, or pain when chewing, because a displaced cheekbone or arch can press on part of the lower jaw.</li>
  <li><strong>Bruising around the eye</strong> and bleeding into the white of the eye, especially on the outer side.</li>
  <li><strong>A step or sharp tenderness</strong> along the lower rim of the eye socket or near the outer end of the eyebrow.</li>
  <li><strong>Double vision</strong>, or one eye appearing lower or more sunken than the other.</li>
  <li><strong>A nosebleed</strong> from one nostril, from blood in the sinus behind the cheek.</li>
  <li>A bite that feels different, if the upper jaw is also involved.</li>
</ul>
<p>As with an eye socket fracture, avoid blowing your nose: a cheekbone fracture often opens into the sinus, and blowing can force air into the tissues around the eye. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/orbital-fracture-eye-socket-symptoms-treatment/">orbital (eye socket) fractures</a> explains why in more detail.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department immediately if:</strong> vision is reduced or lost, the eye is very painful or bulging, there are signs of a head injury (repeated vomiting, drowsiness, confusion, a seizure), clear watery fluid is coming from the nose or ear, breathing is difficult, or bleeding will not stop. After a road accident, neck injury should also be assumed until it has been checked.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="diagnosis">How a cheekbone fracture is diagnosed</h2>
<p>Assessment starts with a careful examination. The surgeon compares the two cheeks, often looking down from above your head, where flattening is easier to see. They feel along the rims of the eye socket, test sensation in the cheek and lip, measure how far your mouth opens, check your bite and examine your eye movements and vision.</p>
<p>A <strong>CT scan</strong>, often with 3D reconstruction, is the standard test. It shows how far the cheekbone has moved, whether the floor of the eye socket is broken and whether other bones are involved. Facial fractures frequently come in combination, so the jaw, nose and teeth are checked too; our article on <a href="/blogs/broken-jaw-symptoms/">the symptoms of a broken jaw</a> covers the signs of an associated jaw fracture. If the eye socket is involved, an eye specialist (ophthalmologist) examines the eye before surgery is planned.</p>

<h2 id="does-it-need-surgery">Does a broken cheekbone always need surgery?</h2>
<p>No. The decision depends on how far the bone has moved and whether it is affecting the eye, your mouth opening or the shape of your face. Surgery is one option among several, and for many undisplaced fractures it is not needed at all.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Type of fracture</th><th>Usual approach</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Undisplaced or minimally displaced, eye and mouth opening normal</td><td>Non-surgical care: soft diet, no nose-blowing, no pressure on the cheek, pain relief</td><td>Reviewed once swelling settles to confirm the cheek has not dropped</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Isolated arch fracture with a dent or limited mouth opening</td><td>Bone lifted back through a small cut in the temple hairline or inside the mouth</td><td>Often holds without plates</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Displaced ZMC (tripod) fracture</td><td>Open reduction and internal fixation with small titanium plates</td><td>Restores cheek shape and eye socket volume</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ZMC fracture with eye socket floor damage</td><td>Plating plus reconstruction of the socket floor</td><td>Considered when there is double vision or a sunken eye</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fracture that has healed out of position</td><td>Planned re-cutting and repositioning, or contour correction</td><td>More complex; results less predictable</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<h3>Why timing matters</h3>
<p>Many surgeons wait a few days for the worst swelling to go down, which makes it easier to judge the cheek shape and to align the bone accurately. Repair is usually carried out within about one to two weeks of the injury. After roughly three weeks the bone starts to knit in its new position, and correcting it may then require re-cutting the bone. Surgery is brought forward if the eye is at risk. When a head injury or other serious injuries come first, facial repair is planned as soon as it is safe.</p>

