<h2 id="surgery">What happens during cheekbone surgery?</h2>
<p>Surgery is done under general anaesthesia in hospital, usually with a stay of one or two nights. Incisions are placed where they are least visible:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Inside the mouth</strong>, above the upper back teeth, which leaves no visible scar.</li>
  <li><strong>At the outer end of the eyebrow</strong> or in the upper eyelid crease, to check and fix the join beside the eye.</li>
  <li><strong>Inside the lower eyelid</strong> or just beneath the lashes, if the floor of the eye socket needs to be examined or repaired.</li>
  <li>For complex fractures involving the arch, occasionally an incision hidden within the hairline across the crown of the head.</li>
</ul>
<p>The cheekbone is lifted back into position, its alignment is checked at several points, and it is held with small titanium plates and screws while it heals. If the socket floor is broken, it is rebuilt at the same time. Planning is based on your CT scan, and for complex injuries 3D planning can help restore symmetry with the uninjured side.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-and-risks">Recovery and risks</h2>
<h3>A typical recovery</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Days 1–3:</strong> swelling and bruising peak. Sleep with your head raised, use wrapped cold compresses as advised and take the medicines prescribed. If you had an incision inside the mouth, rinse gently after meals.</li>
  <li><strong>Around one week:</strong> skin stitches, if any, are removed; stitches inside the mouth dissolve. Many people return to desk work or college after one to two weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 2–6:</strong> eat soft food and chew mainly on the other side, avoid pressure on the cheek (including sleeping on that side) and do not blow your nose for the first two to three weeks. Our guide on <a href="/blogs/what-to-eat-after-jaw-surgery/">what to eat after jaw surgery</a> has practical soft-diet ideas that apply here too.</li>
  <li><strong>Weeks 6–8:</strong> the bone has usually healed enough for most activities; contact sport is often deferred longer.</li>
  <li><strong>Months 3–12:</strong> numbness of the cheek and lip gradually improves in most people, and the last of the swelling settles.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everyone heals differently, and recovery depends on the fracture, other injuries, smoking and general health.</p>
<h3>Risks to discuss with your surgeon</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Some remaining asymmetry, such as a slightly flatter cheek</li>
  <li>Numbness of the cheek, lip or teeth that does not fully recover</li>
  <li>Double vision or a change in eye position, particularly when the socket floor was involved</li>
  <li>Lower eyelid changes after an eyelid incision, such as the lid pulling down slightly</li>
  <li>Infection, or a plate that later needs removal</li>
  <li>Bleeding behind the eye affecting vision, which is rare but serious</li>
  <li>Weakness of the forehead muscles after a hairline incision, which is uncommon and usually temporary</li>
</ul>
<p>Not operating on a displaced fracture also has consequences, including a permanently flat cheek, persistent double vision or restricted mouth opening, so the decision is always a balance that we talk through with you.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>See a maxillofacial surgeon within a day or two of the injury if you notice a flattened cheek, numbness of the cheek or upper lip, difficulty opening your mouth, a change in your bite, or double vision. Seeing a specialist early keeps the full range of options open, including non-surgical care. If you are in Hyderabad and unsure where to go, our guide to <a href="/blogs/emergency-treatment-for-facial-injuries-near-hyderbaad/">emergency treatment for facial injuries</a> explains the first steps.</p>
<p>Our <a href="/facial-injuries">facial injury treatment</a> team sees patients in Gachibowli and operates at NABH-accredited partner hospitals (CARE Hospitals, KIMS, Apollo Spectra and Yashoda). The 24/7 facial-trauma line is <strong>+91 90006 66476</strong>; for any emergency, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department first. Cashless insurance may be possible for accident-related surgery, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation, so keep copies of your emergency notes and CT report.</p>
